1	# Tool Reference
2	
3	Complete documentation for all available Redmine MCP Server tools.
4	
5	## Security Best Practices
6	
7	### SSL/TLS Configuration
8	
9	The Redmine MCP Server supports comprehensive SSL/TLS configuration for secure connections to your Redmine instance.
10	
11	**Recommended Practices:**
12	
13	1. **Always Use HTTPS**
14	   ```bash
15	   # In .env file
16	   REDMINE_URL=https://redmine.company.com  # Use https://, not http://
17	   ```
18	
19	2. **Enable SSL Verification (Default)**
20	   - SSL verification is enabled by default for security
21	   - Never disable in production environments
22	   - Only disable for development/testing when absolutely necessary
23	
24	3. **Self-Signed Certificates**
25	
26	   For Redmine servers with self-signed certificates or internal CA infrastructure:
27	
28	   ```bash
29	   # In .env file
30	   REDMINE_SSL_CERT=/path/to/ca-certificate.crt
31	   ```
32	
33	   **Security Considerations:**
34	   - Verify certificate authenticity before trusting
35	   - Obtain certificates from trusted administrators
36	   - Use absolute paths for certificate files
37	   - Ensure certificate files have appropriate permissions (644)
38	
39	4. **Mutual TLS (Client Certificates)**
40	
41	   For high-security environments requiring client certificate authentication:
42	
43	   ```bash
44	   # In .env file
45	   REDMINE_SSL_CLIENT_CERT=/path/to/cert.pem,/path/to/key.pem
46	   ```
47	
48	   **Security Considerations:**
49	   - Private keys MUST be unencrypted (Python requests library requirement)
50	   - Store private keys securely with restricted permissions (600)

